Ohle Claussen wrote:
> The point is that once the player has found some or all key types, all
> according locks become trivial. Most alternative ways around locks involve
> consumable resources, and can possibly be dangerous. The fun point about
> your proposed additional lock types was the challenge they pose, but once
> all keys are found, that challenge has vanished.

Except there's no guarantee that those keys will even be seen in the
game, indeed the keys should probably be inserted into item generation
by subdividing the generation odds for skeleton keys.  (After all,
there are already lock picks and credit cards to sub for skeleton keys
should one not be found.)  My rule of thumb is, if it's conceivable
that the player could possibly want to wish for a new item (or write
it, or mix for it), but it's not essential or a no-brainer that he do
so, then it's a good addition.  I think the additional key types would
fulfill that requirement.

